{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate a growing graph of hidden invariants—unstated assumptions about state, ordering, and coupling—that exist nowhere in the code or tests but are implicitly relied upon by different components. Each locally correct change is validated against documented and tested behavior, not against the full space of invariants actually depended on, so the probability of violating a hidden invariant grows combinatorially with system size. This invariant density, not code complexity itself, is what makes change difficulty scale superlinearly over time."}
